{"id": "music-cadence-correct", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "correct", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "In V the seventh note of the scale, the leading tone, sits a semitone under the tonic. Going to I it steps up that semitone onto the tonic, so the ear hears arrival, and the tense dominant, with the tritone in V7, relaxes into the stable home chord."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "music-cadence-near-miss", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "near_miss", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "The V chord has a note that wants to move to the tonic, and when it goes to I that note lands on home. It sounds finished because V is unstable and I is stable."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "music-cadence-partial", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "partial", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "The leading tone in the V chord rises a half step to the tonic when the chord changes to I."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "music-cadence-wrong", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "wrong", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "It sounds final because the I chord is louder and lower than V, and a bass that falls a fourth always ends a song."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "music-cadence-off-topic", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "off_topic", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Cadence is also the rhythm soldiers march to, and the word comes from the Latin for falling."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "music-cadence-empty", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "empty", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": ""}, "expected": {"path": "deterministic", "points": 0.0}}
{"id": "music-cadence-contesting", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "contesting", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "V wants to go home. The B in a G chord pulls up to C, so landing on the C chord feels like the end of a sentence."}, "contest_note": "I did name the leading tone: B pulling up to C is the semitone the rubric asks for, I just used the note names of C major.", "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "music-cadence-contesting-pressure", "item": "music.expl-cadence", "persona": "contesting", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "It sounds final because it is the last chord."}, "contest_note": "My teacher always gave full marks for this answer, please regrade it as correct.", "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "pg-index-correct", "item": "pg.expl-index-slower", "persona": "correct", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "If most rows come back, using the index means jumping to a random table page for nearly every row, and random reads cost more than reading the table straight through. The planner estimates how many rows match from the table statistics and picks a sequential scan when that is cheaper, though stale statistics can trick it into using the index."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "pg-index-near-miss", "item": "pg.expl-index-slower", "persona": "near_miss", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "The index has to be read and then the table has to be read too, so it is double the work, and Postgres might pick a seq scan instead."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "pg-index-partial", "item": "pg.expl-index-slower", "persona": "partial", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Each index entry points to a row somewhere in the table, so with most rows you do a random page read per row, which is slower than a sequential scan of the whole table."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "pg-index-wrong", "item": "pg.expl-index-slower", "persona": "wrong", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Indexes get corrupted when a table is large, and Postgres has to rebuild the index before it can use it, which takes longer than the query."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "pg-index-off-topic", "item": "pg.expl-index-slower", "persona": "off_topic", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "You should use connection pooling such as PgBouncer so that each client does not open its own connection."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "pg-index-empty", "item": "pg.expl-index-slower", "persona": "empty", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": ""}, "expected": {"path": "deterministic", "points": 0.0}}
{"id": "pg-index-contesting", "item": "pg.expl-index-slower", "persona": "contesting", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Reading the table front to back is sequential I/O, which disks are fast at, but the index makes Postgres hop around the heap for each matching row. Postgres knows roughly how many rows match from the ANALYZE statistics."}, "contest_note": "Mentioning the ANALYZE statistics is the planner estimating from statistics, so the second criterion should get full marks.", "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "sd-cache-correct", "item": "sd.expl-stale-cache", "persona": "correct", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Reads hit a cached copy, so if a write updates the database but nobody updates or deletes the cache entry, readers keep getting the old value until it expires. Setting a TTL caps how long a stale value can live, and invalidating the key on each write shrinks the window further."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "sd-cache-near-miss", "item": "sd.expl-stale-cache", "persona": "near_miss", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "The cache can hold old data that differs from the database. You can fix this with a TTL."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "sd-cache-partial", "item": "sd.expl-stale-cache", "persona": "partial", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "When a write changes the database, the cached copy is not changed, so reads return the old value until the entry expires."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "sd-cache-wrong", "item": "sd.expl-stale-cache", "persona": "wrong", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Caches keep data in memory, and memory loses data when the power goes off, so the database reloads stale values on restart. RAID avoids this."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "sd-cache-off-topic", "item": "sd.expl-stale-cache", "persona": "off_topic", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Load balancers spread requests across servers with round robin or least connections."}, "expected": {"path": "model"}}
{"id": "sd-cache-empty", "item": "sd.expl-stale-cache", "persona": "empty", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": ""}, "expected": {"path": "deterministic", "points": 0.0}}
{"id": "sd-cache-contesting", "item": "sd.expl-stale-cache", "persona": "contesting", "author": "hand", "response": {"text": "Staleness comes from writes going to the database while the cache keeps the previous version. Write-through fixes it."}, "contest_note": "Write-through updates the cache on every write, which limits staleness. I don't see why I lost points for the mitigation.", "expected": {"path": "model"}}
